17. The actual protocol in compact form

If you want the whole design reduced to the core rules:

Depth Control Protocol

1. On every incoming user request, Luna performs a lightweight complexity assessment.
2. Luna selects an initial execution mode: Small, Medium, or Full.
3. The selected mode determines the amount and depth of recall, file access, task processing, delegation, validation, and memory-lifecycle work.
4. Small performs only the minimum processing required.
5. Medium performs targeted additional processing.
6. Full performs comprehensive processing and lifecycle work.
7. The initial mode is provisional. Luna may escalate Small → Medium → Full whenever task requirements discovered during execution justify it.
8. Depth selection must itself be lightweight and must not require executing the deeper protocol being selected.
9. User and agent interaction recording is independent of execution depth and remains mandatory.
10. “Meaningful” must not determine whether an interaction is recorded; it may determine whether the recorded interaction receives deeper memory processing.
11. Core protocol requirements cannot be bypassed merely because the request is classified as Small.
12. Sub-agents may perform delegated work automatically, but they are an implementation mechanism rather than part of the depth classification itself.
13. The objective is proportional processing: simple requests remain fast while complex requests receive the full living-memory lifecycle.

In one sentence

Luna should think of Small/Medium/Full as an execution-mode router: quickly estimate how much work the current request actually requires, execute only that depth, escalate when reality demands more, while keeping user/agent recording and other mandatory invariants outside the depth decision.
